data.content:
The lightest option is the built-in **`TimeProvider`** class (available since .NET 8). No packages, no DI container needed — just accept it as an optional constructor parameter:

```csharp
namespace InvoiceLib;

public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? timeProvider = null)
    {
        _time = timeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System;
    }

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

In tests, use `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` (a small NuGet package) or hand-roll a fake:

```csharp
var fakeTime = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fakeTime);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 14, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

**Why this is the lightest path:**
- `TimeProvider` is in the BCL — zero production dependencies
- The default parameter (`= null`) means existing callers don't break
- No interface to define, no DI container to wire up
- You can advance/freeze time deterministically in tests

